<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Ceuta</h1><p class="st-meta">1061 CE – 1084 CE (approx.) · capital Ceuta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A taifa across the Strait in Ceuta and Tangier, founded by a former Hammudid slave commander and the last obstacle to Almoravid control of the Strait.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> had been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudid</a> possession. Around 1061 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suqut-al-barghawati">Suqut al-Barghawati</a>, a commander of slave origin who had served the Hammudids, made himself independent there and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, ruling the African shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suqut-al-barghawati">Suqut</a> controlled the vital ports linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> and maintained ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a>, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-seville">Taifa of Seville</a>. His state was essentially a military regime defending the coast against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a>, which was expanding across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> in 1077, killing the aged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suqut-al-barghawati">Suqut</a> in battle, and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> from his son around 1083-1084. Control of Ceuta allowed them to cross into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in 1086.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1061 CE – 1084 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.82%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1061 CE – 1084 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ceuta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Suqut al-Barghawati</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Barghawatid Ceuta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1072&amp;focus=taifa-of-ceuta" aria-label="Taifa of Ceuta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4546 882 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Ceuta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4866 1138L4889 1110M4866 1138L4799 1289"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4889 1110h0M4799 1289h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4866 1138h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4866 1138h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1072&amp;focus=taifa-of-ceuta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
